+# Keep sessions in the web process
+
+For maintainers changing sessions or deploying the web app: sessions now live in the web process because volunteers
+asked to remove the Postgres session query from requests.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres was chosen to keep sessions through deploys without
+adding a service. Volunteers have now requested a process-local dictionary to make requests faster by removing
+session queries. No timing measurements accompany this change.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: can keep a member on the same process, but cannot preserve its sessions
+  through restarts.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost on the second force, a service the volunteers would run only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: chosen previously for shared, durable sessions; replaced to remove session queries from requests.
+- **Process-local dictionary**: chosen to remove those queries without adding a service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions live in a dictionary in the web process, exposed by `larder/session_store.py`. This removes the database
+round trip for session storage without adding a service.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session access no longer queries Postgres. Processes do not share sessions, and restarting a process loses its
+sessions. Existing Postgres sessions are not migrated. Operational workarounds are described in the
+[session limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits).
